# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hyperion backend configuration
# Copy to `.env` and adjust. All values are optional; defaults are shown.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# Address the HTTP server binds to.
#
# SECURITY: this app has NO authentication by design (single trusted user).
# Anything that can reach this port can read every stored provider key.
# Keep this at 127.0.0.1 unless you know exactly what you are doing.
#
# For remote access, install Tailscale and bind to the machine's *Tailscale*
# interface address (e.g. 100.x.y.z) — NOT 0.0.0.0. That exposes the app only
# to your own private mesh network, not to the LAN or the internet.
HYPERION_BIND=127.0.0.1

# Refuse-to-start guard: binding to 0.0.0.0 (all interfaces) is blocked unless
# this is explicitly set to 1. Leave it unset. It exists so that exposing the
# app to every network is always a deliberate act, never a typo.
# HYPERION_ALLOW_ANY_BIND=0

HYPERION_PORT=4317

# Extra hostnames the API will answer to, comma-separated.
#
# The Host guard (src/http/networkGuard.ts) refuses any request whose `Host`
# header is not loopback (127.0.0.1 / localhost / [::1], on any port), the
# HYPERION_BIND address, or one of these. That is what stops DNS rebinding: a
# page on an attacker's domain that resolves to 127.0.0.1 is same-origin with
# this API as far as the browser is concerned, and the Host header is the only
# thing that tells the two apart. It is NOT authentication.
#
# Put your Tailscale name and/or address here if you reach Hyperion remotely.
# Ports are ignored — the name is what matters.
# HYPERION_ALLOWED_HOSTS=hyperion.tailnet.ts.net,100.80.1.2

# Origins allowed to send POST/PUT/PATCH/DELETE, comma-separated.
#
# Defaults to the Vite dev server on both loopback spellings. The API's own
# origins are always allowed and do not need listing. A request with no Origin
# header at all (curl, scripts, tests) is allowed — a browser always sends one
# on these methods, so this only ever refuses a browser. `Origin: null` is
# refused.
#
# Ports and scheme are exact on purpose: there is deliberately no "any loopback
# port" rule, so another app you happen to be running locally cannot drive this
# API from a page. Reaching the UI over Tailscale needs the origin you type in
# the address bar listed here as well as the name in HYPERION_ALLOWED_HOSTS:
#   HYPERION_APP_ORIGINS=http://hyperion.tailnet.ts.net:4318,http://127.0.0.1:4318,http://localhost:4318
# HYPERION_APP_ORIGINS=http://127.0.0.1:4318,http://localhost:4318

# Root directory for the database, encryption key, attachments, projects,
# skills and backups. Defaults to ~/Hyperion
# HYPERION_HOME=C:\Users\you\Hyperion

# Read into `env.nodeEnv`, and `env.isTest` when it is exactly "test".
#
# Nothing in src/ branches on either value today — they are exposed so a future
# feature has one place to ask, rather than reading process.env directly. Two
# things outside src/ do set it: ecosystem.config.cjs sets "production", and
# test/helpers.ts sets "test". Leave it unset for ordinary local development.
#
# Do not use it to switch behaviour that security depends on. The bind guard,
# the fsScope roots and the secret redaction are unconditional on purpose; a
# feature that weakens itself when NODE_ENV says "development" is one typo in a
# service file away from doing that in production.
# NODE_ENV=development

# Base URL probed when detecting the local Ollama runner.
OLLAMA_URL=http://localhost:11434

# Runtime health-check tuning (auto-failover).
# A provider is marked unhealthy after N consecutive failures inside the window.
HYPERION_HEALTH_FAILURE_THRESHOLD=2
HYPERION_HEALTH_WINDOW_MS=20000

# How long a provider's model list is cached before it is re-fetched.
HYPERION_MODEL_CACHE_TTL_MS=300000

# Per-request upstream timeout for provider calls.
HYPERION_REQUEST_TIMEOUT_MS=120000

# Storage cleanup thresholds (days).
# Pinned assets are exempt. Pinned AND archived chats are exempt — archiving
# means "keep this, just out of my way", not "on its way to deletion".
# The chat TTL is deliberately 120 days, not the 30 the spec first suggested.
# A chat is the record of a decision, and those get looked up months later.
HYPERION_ASSET_TTL_DAYS=90
HYPERION_CHAT_TTL_DAYS=120

# Set to 1 to disable the background cleanup job entirely.
# HYPERION_CLEANUP_DISABLED=0

# --- local provider detection ------------------------------------------------
# Probing for a local runner (Ollama) is a one-time answer, not a background
# scan: "not detected" is reported immediately rather than retried forever.
# The attempts below exist only for a runner that *is* there but answers badly
# — a connection reset or a cold start — not for one that is absent.
HYPERION_LOCAL_DETECT_ATTEMPTS=3
HYPERION_LOCAL_DETECT_TIMEOUT_MS=2500

# --- uploads -----------------------------------------------------------------
# Largest single upload accepted, in bytes (default 100 MB). Oversized requests
# are refused as `bad_request` with the 413 status preserved.
# HYPERION_MAX_UPLOAD_BYTES=104857600

# --- scheduler and automatic backups -----------------------------------------
# Set to 1 to disable the automatic daily backup AND the scheduled-task runner.
# This is the switch the test helper uses: a suite that boots the app must not
# start firing real Cowork runs against your providers.
# HYPERION_SCHEDULER_DISABLED=0

# How many automatic backups to keep. Unbounded retention turns a safety feature
# into the thing that fills the disk.
HYPERION_AUTO_BACKUP_KEEP=7

# An extra folder each automatic backup is COPIED into — point it at a Drive,
# Dropbox or OneDrive sync folder and the archive leaves the machine on its own.
# A backup that only ever exists on the disk holding the database is not really a
# backup: the failure it protects against destroys both at once.
#
# The archive is still written to ~/Hyperion/backups first and listed, inspected,
# imported and pruned from there — this is a copy, not a move. If the copy fails
# (drive unmounted, folder full) the backup itself still succeeded; the failure is
# logged as a warning.
#
# Unset by default. There is deliberately no API or settings key for this: it is
# the one path outside the app's own data root, so it is settable only by whoever
# controls the environment, never by an HTTP request.
# HYPERION_BACKUP_DEST=C:\Users\you\My Drive\Hyperion backups


# How long a due scheduled task waits for the app-wide job lock before recording
# itself `skipped`. Fifteen minutes because the collision this exists for is a
# user mid-chat, which resolves in minutes; a task still blocked after that is
# competing with something long-running, and silently starting it an hour late is
# worse than reporting that it did not run.
HYPERION_SCHEDULED_TASK_WAIT_MS=900000

# --- memory plugin (mem0, self-hosted) ---------------------------------------
# Off by default — the toggle is the `plugins.memory.enabled` setting, not an env
# var. These only describe *how* memory runs once it is switched on.
#
# mem0 needs its own small LLM and an embedding model to extract and index facts.
# The defaults point at Ollama, so a stock local install works with no keys and
# nothing leaves the machine. They are deliberately separate from the chat
# provider: memory extraction is a background chore and should not burn the good
# model's quota.
HYPERION_MEMORY_PROVIDER=ollama
HYPERION_MEMORY_LLM_MODEL=llama3.1:8b
HYPERION_MEMORY_EMBED_MODEL=nomic-embed-text

# Must match the embedding model above. Changing the model without changing the
# dimensions leaves an index that cannot be queried.
HYPERION_MEMORY_EMBED_DIMS=768

# How many recalled facts may be injected into a single turn. A short set, never
# a dump of everything the app remembers.
HYPERION_MEMORY_RETRIEVE_LIMIT=5


# --- stored images -----------------------------------------------------------
# Longest edge (pixels) an image may keep once stored. Larger images are
# downscaled once on the way to disk; smaller ones are stored untouched.
# 1568 is about where vision models stop gaining from extra resolution, so
# anything beyond it costs upload time, disk and tokens for nothing.
# Set to 0 to store every image exactly as it arrived.
HYPERION_IMAGE_MAX_EDGE=1568

# --- skill dependencies ------------------------------------------------------
# When a skill declares dependencies (requirements.txt, pyproject.toml,
# package.json, or a SKILL.md `dependencies:` list), they are installed once at
# intake into an environment scoped to that skill alone — never into Hyperion's
# own environment.

# Skip installation entirely. Skills that declare dependencies will report
# `failed` and can be provisioned later via POST /api/skills/:id/provision.
# Useful offline, or in CI.
# HYPERION_SKILL_DEPS_DISABLED=0

# Cap on a single install command. The default allows for a slow wheel build.
# HYPERION_SKILL_DEPS_TIMEOUT_MS=600000

# Full paths to the interpreters used for provisioning. Set these when `python3`
# or `npm` is not on the PATH of the process running Hyperion (common when it is
# started by a service manager rather than a shell).
# HYPERION_SKILL_DEPS_PYTHON=
# HYPERION_SKILL_DEPS_NPM=

# --- Code task parallelism ---------------------------------------------------
# Everything else in this app runs under one app-wide job lock: one chat, one
# backup, one skill intake at a time. The Code tool is the exception. It gets its
# own counting lane, so several coding tasks can run at once while still not
# competing with the rest of the app.
#
# How many Code tasks may run concurrently. Each one is a real agent doing real
# work — file reads, commands, model calls — so this is a throughput knob, not a
# free win. 2 is a comfortable default; raise it only if the machine has the
# cores and the provider has the rate limit.
# When the lane is full a new task is refused immediately with `busy` rather than
# being silently queued behind work the caller cannot see.
HYPERION_CODE_LANE_LIMIT=2

# Each Code task runs in its own git worktree under ~/Hyperion/worktrees/<task>/
# on a branch named `hyperion-task-<task>`, so two tasks editing the same
# repository never see each other's half-finished files. The worktree shares the
# repository's object store, so this is cheap — no second clone.
#
# On completion the worktree is removed, but only when it holds nothing you would
# miss: a dirty tree or commits that are not on the base commit are always kept,
# regardless of this setting. Set to 1 to keep every worktree, including clean
# ones — useful when debugging what an agent actually did.
# HYPERION_CODE_KEEP_WORKTREES=0
#
# If git is missing, or the target is not a git repository, Code does not fail —
# it falls back to working directly in the project directory and says so in the
# task's `started` event. Isolation degrades; the task still runs.

# The port the browser preview is served on (src/http/previewServer.ts).
#
# This setting governs the Code Canvas preview only. Design's sandboxed preview
# is a different mechanism on a different port — a route on the API itself
# (GET /api/design/preview/:versionId, docs/API.md §23) with scripts off — and
# is unaffected by this value.
#
# The preview is a *second HTTP server*, not a route on the API, and the second
# origin is the isolation: a page an agent wrote, served from the API's own
# origin, could fetch /api/settings and read every stored provider key, and no
# CSP would stop it because CSP constrains what a document may do, not what its
# origin may read. See docs/CONTRACTS.md, "One path seam, and a second origin".
#
# 0 disables the feature: POST /api/code/tasks/:taskId/preview answers
# 403 capability_disabled with details.setting=HYPERION_PREVIEW_PORT, and the
# rest of the app is unaffected. A port already in use degrades the same way at
# boot — the feature is off, boot is not.
#
# If you reach Hyperion over Tailscale or another proxy, the preview hostname
# must also be listed in HYPERION_ALLOWED_HOSTS: the listener runs the same Host
# guard as the API. Do NOT add the preview port to HYPERION_APP_ORIGINS — that
# list is who may send state-changing requests to the API, and a preview origin
# must never be one of them.
#
# The listener itself speaks plain HTTP, so behind a gateway that terminates TLS
# it reads x-forwarded-proto and x-forwarded-host to name the browser's real
# origin in the CSP (the forwarded host only after the same allow-list check the
# Host header passed). Nothing needs configuring for that; it matters because the
# explicit origin is what serves the page when `'self'` does not match under the
# sandbox's opaque origin.
HYPERION_PREVIEW_PORT=4320

# error | warn | info | debug
HYPERION_LOG_LEVEL=info
